  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nStrong mid level ridge extending across the region will start to\nweaken and shift southwards a bit tomorrow. Temperatures will rise\ninto the lower to mid 90s today with the hottest readings across\nnorthern counties. That will push a bit southward tomorrow. Heat \nindex forecast is right around the advisory threshold of 100 both \ndays. The heat advisory already issued for today seems to \nappropriately cover locations with the greater potential to reach or \nexceed 100 beyond just isolated spots. With the forecast tomorrow \nright around the threshold, opted to allow for another look at new \ndata later today to see if that resolves if/where an advisory might \nbe needed on Thursday.\n\nIncreasing clouds and convection will keep air temperatures from\nbeing quite so high on Friday and especially Saturday. However, the\ndew point will be higher in a very moist environment. So the heat\nindex will still be in the mid to upper 90s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nA pattern change will occur heading into the end of the week as a\ntrough over eastern Canada deepens and eventually extends across the\neastern US. Friday will be a transition day with a weak short wave\ncoming out of the central Plains into the lower Great Lakes/mid Ohio\nValley between stronger flow to the north and the departing high.\nA reservoir of deep moisture will get drawn north and then east with\nprecipitable water increasing to 1.8 to 2.0 inches. It will be \nunstable with CAPE approaching 2000 J/kg in western counties. So \nshowers and thunderstorms will develop into the area through the day.\n\nStronger short wave dropping out of the Canadian Plains will track\nacross the Great Lakes Saturday into Saturday night. The area will be\nslightly more unstable than Friday. This will result in more showers\nand storms through the area with locally heavy rainfall possible. \nThere could be some lingering precipitation into Sunday as an \nassociated front crosses the area.",
